739
00:34:53,000 --> 00:34:54,000
Okay.

740
00:34:54,000 --> 00:34:55,000
All right.

741
00:34:55,000 --> 00:34:57,000
Yeah, I will say my daughter, she just went through sixth grade.

742
00:34:57,000 --> 00:34:58,000
She's getting ready to be in seventh grade.

743
00:34:58,000 --> 00:35:03,000
And if I could describe the first middle school year, it was very rushed.

744
00:35:03,000 --> 00:35:05,000
She struggled heavily.

745
00:35:05,000 --> 00:35:09,000
And I mean the middle school team, you all did an amazing job, but it was still, it was rushed.

746
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:14,000
The amount of time that she had between classes, having to keep up with things, it was very challenging.

747
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:16,000
I mean she made it, but it was very challenging for her.

748
00:35:16,000 --> 00:35:29,000
So I know I've been talking with her a lot about this and kind of talked to her about this and she seemed very excited that she would have some more consistency throughout her semester as opposed to having to keep up with something, a million classes between A and B days.

749
00:35:29,000 --> 00:35:30,000
So I appreciate you getting that feedback.

750
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:35,000
We get that a lot from parents and I mean it's already a huge challenge going from elementary

751
00:35:35,000 --> 00:35:38,000
school to middle school in a number of different ways.

752
00:35:38,000 --> 00:35:42,000
Elementary, as you can imagine, it's their primary teacher and then also going to their unified

753
00:35:42,000 --> 00:35:43,000
arts and visiting and whatnot.

754
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:50,000
But when they come to middle school, we do try to, I'll say, hold them by the hand a little bit at the beginning to get them easing through the transition.

755
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:56,000
But even when the training wheels are off, it's just, it's managing a lot as 11, 12 year old student.

756
00:35:56,000 --> 00:36:00,000
And I'll focus on the six graders, like your daughter, you said, it's just a lot to manage.

757
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:08,000
So if we can be more consistent with it, especially by adding more consistency with Windblock, I feel like it could help overall student outcomes as well.

758
00:36:08,000 --> 00:36:11,000
Dawn and then Shauna.

759
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:21,000
Just how long are the courses that they, for instance, would computer tech, math, social studies, and science all meet for the same amount of time?

760
00:36:21,000 --> 00:36:22,000
Yeah.

761
00:36:22,000 --> 00:36:26,000
One of the things we love about our schedule is it's broken up so it's even across the board.

762
00:36:26,000 --> 00:36:27,000
Fifty minutes or so?

763
00:36:27,000 --> 00:36:28,000
Forty eight.

764
00:36:28,000 --> 00:36:29,000
Yes.

765
00:36:29,000 --> 00:36:31,000
We put them in two minutes.

766
00:36:31,000 --> 00:36:32,000
Hey, every minute counts.

767
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:33,000
Yes, it does.

768
00:36:33,000 --> 00:36:37,000
Every single minute is we put in two minutes for passing time.

769
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:38,000
So passing time.

770
00:36:38,000 --> 00:36:39,000
Yep.

771
00:36:39,000 --> 00:36:42,000
At one point it used to be that it ended at the same time.

772
00:36:42,000 --> 00:36:49,000
Except for going for fee or, you know, whatever might be outside of the area that the team is.

773
00:36:49,000 --> 00:36:54,000
They plan out and come back because we actually, I don't love having bells in a school.

774
00:36:54,000 --> 00:36:55,000
It's not my preferred method.

775
00:36:55,000 --> 00:37:01,000
However, there's something to be said for class is over, two minutes and then class starts so that we all know the time to be there.

776
00:37:01,000 --> 00:37:04,000
And two minutes is necessary at one point.

777
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:06,000
We've had many schedules over time.

778
00:37:06,000 --> 00:37:09,000
They used to be ended at this time and also start at the same time.

779
00:37:09,000 --> 00:37:11,000
And nobody was transporting or teleporting.

780
00:37:11,000 --> 00:37:16,000
So I was like, maybe we should give them two minutes.

781
00:37:16,000 --> 00:37:19,000
So yeah, every minute is very valuable.

782
00:37:19,000 --> 00:37:27,000
So I'm sadly no longer a middle school parent anymore because now both of my kids are in high school.

