00:50:35,000 --> 00:50:36,000
No.

993
00:50:36,000 --> 00:50:37,000
Okay.

994
00:50:37,000 --> 00:50:38,000
I'm keeping it adopted.

995
00:50:38,000 --> 00:50:40,000
Justin, did you want to close up?

996
00:50:40,000 --> 00:50:43,000
I just want to share and make sure the board has all the information that

997
00:50:43,000 --> 00:50:47,000
where I stand on this because I did share it with the curriculum instruction committee.

998
00:50:47,000 --> 00:50:50,000
So I just want to share that same information tonight.

999
00:50:50,000 --> 00:50:57,000
I do not think it is the purview of the board to approve and adopt daily building schedules on an annual basis.

1000
00:50:57,000 --> 00:51:03,000
I do think that there is absolutely a way for people informed and weigh in, but I do this.

1001
00:51:03,000 --> 00:51:07,000
I do view this as the operational annual review of schedules.

1002
00:51:07,000 --> 00:51:13,000
I'm talking with staff and kids so we can design schedules year to year as we're a constant state of improvement.

1003
00:51:13,000 --> 00:51:18,000
So I want all the members here to understand that position and I share that with the committee that was there.

1004
00:51:18,000 --> 00:51:19,000
Yeah.

1005
00:51:19,000 --> 00:51:20,000
I like what you said there.

1006
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:21,000
I don't...

1007
00:51:21,000 --> 00:51:22,000
I just...

1008
00:51:22,000 --> 00:51:25,000
I like the work that was done here, especially for me.

1009
00:51:25,000 --> 00:51:37,000
This is meaningful because I, you can upset me in an executive functioning capacity pretty easily by messing up my schedule or by giving me too, like a scattered...

1010
00:51:37,000 --> 00:51:38,000
You know, I need essentialism.

1011
00:51:38,000 --> 00:51:39,000
Like I need...

1012
00:51:39,000 --> 00:51:40,000
What am I doing?

1013
00:51:40,000 --> 00:51:41,000
Use your schedule.

1014
00:51:41,000 --> 00:51:43,000
If you go in too many...

1015
00:51:43,000 --> 00:51:44,000
So I like the ideas.

1016
00:51:44,000 --> 00:51:45,000
The ideas are great.

1017
00:51:45,000 --> 00:51:46,000
In terms of what we're talking about right now.

1018
00:51:46,000 --> 00:51:47,000
I don't know.

1019
00:51:47,000 --> 00:51:48,000
Yeah.

1020
00:51:48,000 --> 00:51:52,000
I agree with what Justin just said.

1021
00:51:52,000 --> 00:51:59,000
I think it was a curriculum change or books, everything else I think, but I would more rather...

1022
00:51:59,000 --> 00:52:02,000
I don't know enough about this at the level that you guys do.

1023
00:52:02,000 --> 00:52:05,000
If it stinks and we're getting a lot of bad feedback, that's when this...

1024
00:52:05,000 --> 00:52:08,000
Well, and the superintendent hasn't addressed that.

1025
00:52:08,000 --> 00:52:10,000
I think we get involved.

1026
00:52:10,000 --> 00:52:14,000
Now we're talking a different story, but I don't fear that in this case.

1027
00:52:14,000 --> 00:52:15,000
I think this is...

1028
00:52:15,000 --> 00:52:21,000
I would love to empower the people that put this together to do it and drive this and see what positive changes we have.

1029
00:52:21,000 --> 00:52:22,000
It looks good to me.

1030
00:52:22,000 --> 00:52:23,000
John.

1031
00:52:23,000 --> 00:52:28,000
I do agree with Justin in that.

1032
00:52:28,000 --> 00:52:35,000
And maybe not with you in this case, because I don't think that it's our job to do your job.

1033
00:52:35,000 --> 00:52:36,000
No.

1034
00:52:36,000 --> 00:52:43,000
It's our job to give you feedback when you get a presentation and there are aspects of it that...

1035
00:52:43,000 --> 00:52:49,000
you bother us or we feel aren't fully expressed or we need more information about it to work on something.

1036
00:52:49,000 --> 00:52:51,000
We would ask you to do that.

1037
00:52:51,000 --> 00:53:05,000
And generally, you know, that type of feedback is important for you to get, you know, a sense of what's going on in terms of the public, you know, response to what's going on in the schools, which we represent.

1038
00:53:05,000 --> 00:53:06,000
That's important.

1039
00:53:06,000 --> 00:53:20,000
And I think they can really slow down progress in the district if the superintendent isn't the CEO who's running the show and ensuring that you're doing the work that you have demonstrated you've done.

1040
00:53:20,000 --> 00:53:24,000
You've had a good process and you're comfortable explaining it.

1041
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:26,000
You seem to have supportive staff.

1042
00:53:26,000 --> 00:53:30,000
It's for the benefit of students and it's nothing outrageous.

1043
00:53:30,000 --> 00:53:33,000
It's actually just to kind of a...

1044
00:53:33,000 --> 00:53:34,000
Don't say tweet.

1045
00:53:34,000 --> 00:53:47,000
...in order to provide time for the kids who need it most, to have people have access to, you know, their schedules, to be a part of, you know, helping them on a daily basis.

1046
00:53:47,000 --> 00:53:54,000
So, you know, it's solid and it's great that you're here to tell us that.

1047
00:53:54,000 --> 00:54:02,000
So I would be very happy to, you know, do a vote, but I think it would be kind of irrelevant.

1048
00:54:02,000 --> 00:54:10,000
And I think that to show support, we could all show support or show the degree of support that we have.

1049
00:54:10,000 --> 00:54:11,000
You have that information.

1050
00:54:11,000 --> 00:54:13,000
That's kind of where I'm at.

1051
00:54:13,000 --> 00:54:14,000
Yeah.

1052
00:54:14,000 --> 00:54:15,000
Jack.

1053
00:54:15,000 --> 00:54:16,000
Yeah.

1054
00:54:16,000 --> 00:54:18,000
And I'll just finish up with this.

1055
00:54:18,000 --> 00:54:26,000
It is a change in the delivery of the curriculum that affects every student in the school.

1056
00:54:26,000 --> 00:54:27,000
We are not...

1057
00:54:27,000 --> 00:54:32,000
Once we vote on it, which I believe it would be approved, then it's up to Justice and Nicole.

1058
00:54:32,000 --> 00:54:38,000
Just like when we change your vote, just like when we do anything else in here, we have...

1059
00:54:38,000 --> 00:54:39,000
If we...

1060
00:54:39,000 --> 00:54:43,000
I'm afraid if we give up some of this authority, which I believe we have...

1061
00:54:43,000 --> 00:54:49,000
And again, you could argue that this is a gray issue, but it is a change.

1062
00:54:49,000 --> 00:54:50,000
Don just said it.

1063
00:54:50,000 --> 00:54:55,000
That substantially impacts the students positively.

1064
00:54:55,000 --> 00:54:58,000
Every single student in that middle school.

1065
00:54:58,000 --> 00:55:02,000
Why wouldn't we vote to approve that?

1066
00:55:02,000 --> 00:55:05,000
We do it at every single meeting.

1067
00:55:05,000 --> 00:55:08,000
And I just...

1068
00:55:08,000 --> 00:55:12,000
Occasionally, I mean, a few of you know how I feel about some of this stuff.

1069
00:55:12,000 --> 00:55:14,000
Ultimately, we're responsible.

1070
00:55:14,000 --> 00:55:16,000
Ultimately, it is us.

1071
00:55:16,000 --> 00:55:20,000
And we have oversight over the superintendent.

1072
00:55:20,000 --> 00:55:26,000
I don't mean in a dictatorial kind of way, but I mean, that's the way it works.

1073
00:55:26,000 --> 00:55:32,000
This is more of a vote of confidence on the work that they did than anything else.

1074
00:55:32,000 --> 00:55:34,000
But if you don't...

1075
00:55:34,000 --> 00:55:38,000
If you think this is just a tweak, then why are we even having this discussion?

1076
00:55:38,000 --> 00:55:40,000
It's just a scheduling change, but it's not.

1077
00:55:40,000 --> 00:55:52,000
And so I think it's a change, a substantial change in the delivery of the curriculum that needs our approval,

1078
00:55:52,000 --> 00:55:56,000
because that's proper governance, in my opinion.

1079
00:55:56,000 --> 00:55:57,000
Don.

1080
00:55:57,000 --> 00:55:59,000
And then Mary Ann.

1081
00:55:59,000 --> 00:56:06,000
You know, one of the things that was most important that he asked when they did evaluations of schools and districts,

1082
00:56:06,000 --> 00:56:12,000
you know, was the degree to which the educators had autonomy, the ability, you know,

1083
00:56:12,000 --> 00:56:21,000
to make decisions based on their background, their experience, and what they can demonstrate as best practice.

1084
00:56:21,000 --> 00:56:26,000
And boards should be supportive of that work in general.

1085
00:56:26,000 --> 00:56:32,000
If there is an issue that needs to be voiced, the board is where it's going to happen.

1086
00:56:32,000 --> 00:56:41,000
But once we hire a superintendent, they're in a position to be that person to take a look at what the change is.

1087
00:56:41,000 --> 00:56:50,000
Coach the group, if he sees group halls, things that aren't well-expressed or things that aren't going to be clear in the public,

1088
00:56:50,000 --> 00:56:55,000
that, you know, basically that he doesn't agree with, you know, that's where his experience, expertise,

1089
00:56:55,000 --> 00:56:59,000
that's why we hire somebody there, the best person we can find.

1090
00:56:59,000 --> 00:57:04,000
And we've done searches, you know, and been very picky about who we choose.

1091
00:57:04,000 --> 00:57:06,000
And we struggle sometimes.

1092
00:57:06,000 --> 00:57:07,000
But you know what?

1093
00:57:07,000 --> 00:57:14,000
If you don't respect that process, you're going to lose the faith of the people that are doing the work in the district.

1094
00:57:14,000 --> 00:57:16,000
And I would hate to see that happen.

1095
00:57:16,000 --> 00:57:17,000
Very good.

1096
00:57:17,000 --> 00:57:21,000
I have a procedural question.

1097
00:57:21,000 --> 00:57:33,000
How does one support a program that is up as a motion to be adopted when you don't believe that the adoption is appropriate?

1098
00:57:33,000 --> 00:57:35,000
So this is what could happen.

1099
00:57:35,000 --> 00:57:40,000
One, Mark can withdraw a second, and if nobody else seconds it, the motion dies.

1100
00:57:40,000 --> 00:57:43,000
And then if someone wants to make a motion for support, that's fine.

1101
00:57:43,000 --> 00:57:47,000
Or if people just want to say, hey, we support you, go forth, that's also fine.

1102
00:57:47,000 --> 00:57:51,000
If Mark doesn't withdraw a second, Jack won't amend his motion.

1103
00:57:51,000 --> 00:57:55,000
If you don't believe it, you can say, hey, I'm voting no, not because I don't believe in what you're doing.

1104
00:57:55,000 --> 00:57:56,000
I don't believe in the motion.

1105
00:57:56,000 --> 00:57:57,000
It can fail.

1106
00:57:57,000 --> 00:58:03,000
And then if you want to make a motion for support or just say, hey, we support you all, go forth, you can do it that way.

1107
00:58:03,000 --> 00:58:05,000
So those are the two pathways.

1108
00:58:05,000 --> 00:58:06,000
Go ahead, Mark.

1109
00:58:06,000 --> 00:58:14,000
Jack, would you be willing to change the language in such a way that becomes more nebulous so that we can leave this for a further discussion?

1110
00:58:14,000 --> 00:58:18,000
I believe the board in general is supportive.

1111
00:58:18,000 --> 00:58:22,000
We want to show that support, the work they've done, the super is done.

1112
00:58:22,000 --> 00:58:25,000
Again, this is a different philosophical question.

1113
00:58:25,000 --> 00:58:33,000
I don't want the nuances of this to sidetrack or give us an off ramp that we don't want.

1114
00:58:33,000 --> 00:58:36,000
Would you be willing to change the verbiage of your motion?

1115
00:58:36,000 --> 00:58:38,000
I understand where you're coming from, Mark.

1116
00:58:38,000 --> 00:58:40,000
I just want to read this one last thing.

1117
00:58:40,000 --> 00:58:43,000
I kind of wrote it down, so I might as well read it.

1118
00:58:43,000 --> 00:58:46,000
The superintendent has the authority to manage the district.

1119
00:58:46,000 --> 00:58:48,000
No one's, nobody questions that.

1120
00:58:48,000 --> 00:58:49,000
I don't question that.

1121
00:58:49,000 --> 00:58:53,000
Is that, is my time up?

1122
00:58:53,000 --> 00:58:54,000
Yes.

1123
00:58:54,000 --> 00:58:57,000
The board has the responsibility to govern it.

1124
00:58:57,000 --> 00:59:02,000
When a proposal fundamentally changes the educational program for every middle school student,

1125
00:59:02,000 --> 00:59:09,000
governance requires that the board have the opportunity to review the rationale, evaluate the educational impact,

1126
00:59:09,000 --> 00:59:14,000
and determine whether the change aligns with the district's goals and policies,

1127
00:59:14,000 --> 00:59:23,000
which I believe it does before the implementation by a vote representing the voice of the people they represent.

1128
00:59:23,000 --> 00:59:24,000
This is about oversight.

1129
00:59:24,000 --> 00:59:29,000
This isn't about controlling what the superintendent does day to day.

1130
00:59:29,000 --> 00:59:36,000
And basically, if we approve this, which I think we would by a vote, we're sending, we're saying, good job.

1131
00:59:36,000 --> 00:59:38,000
We're saying, go at it.

1132
00:59:38,000 --> 00:59:46,000
But not to do that is, it flies in the face of the history of the 20 years I've been over here.

1133
00:59:46,000 --> 00:59:48,000
So, what were you thinking, Marcus, for an amendment?

1134
00:59:48,000 --> 00:59:50,000
Because you know what an open-minded guy is.

1135
00:59:50,000 --> 00:59:54,000
The board is supportive of the schedule as presented.

1136
00:59:54,000 --> 00:59:55,000
We're supportive.

1137
00:59:55,000 --> 00:59:56,000
Yeah.

1138
00:59:56,000 --> 00:59:57,000
Change, adopt, to support.

1139
00:59:57,000 --> 00:59:59,000
If they want to change another thing, do they have to come to us again?

1140
00:59:59,000 --> 01:00:00,000
Like, I don't want to even be part of that.

1141
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:01,000
Right.

1142
01:00:01,000 --> 01:00:04,000
The point you just read doesn't talk about voting to…

1143
01:00:04,000 --> 01:00:07,000
Well, I mean, everybody has a boss.

1144
01:00:07,000 --> 01:00:09,000
Everyone has to answer to somebody.

1145
01:00:09,000 --> 01:00:10,000
Yeah, and they…

1146
01:00:10,000 --> 01:00:14,000
It's fairly asking you to vote, to maybe vote to support.

1147
01:00:14,000 --> 01:00:15,000
Not…

1148
01:00:15,000 --> 01:00:16,000
Adopt.

1149
01:00:16,000 --> 01:00:21,000
Are you willing to change the verbiage to support, Jack?

1150
01:00:21,000 --> 01:00:22,000
That's what you read.

1151
01:00:22,000 --> 01:00:27,000
That's what you used as a, you know, as you're trying to make your point.

1152
01:00:27,000 --> 01:00:28,000
That we have to…

1153
01:00:28,000 --> 01:00:33,000
We have a responsibility to lend our voice to determine whether or not we support it.

1154
01:00:33,000 --> 01:00:34,000
No, we really need…

1155
01:00:34,000 --> 01:00:37,000
They need to know what direction they're going because we need to…

1156
01:00:37,000 --> 01:00:38,000
Well, they're going to go in that…

1157
01:00:38,000 --> 01:00:39,000
Either way, they're going to go in that direction.

1158
01:00:39,000 --> 01:00:40,000
Okay.

1159
01:00:40,000 --> 01:00:41,000
So, vote.

1160
01:00:41,000 --> 01:00:43,000
So, the whole thing is about not having to vote.

1161
01:00:43,000 --> 01:00:44,000
Yes.

1162
01:00:44,000 --> 01:00:45,000
Okay.

1163
01:00:45,000 --> 01:00:46,000
So, do you…

1164
01:00:46,000 --> 01:00:47,000
Okay.

1165
01:00:47,000 --> 01:00:48,000
You know what?

1166
01:00:48,000 --> 01:00:49,000
Let's do support.

1167
01:00:49,000 --> 01:00:50,000
I will amend that to support.

1168
01:00:50,000 --> 01:00:51,000
Okay.

1169
01:00:51,000 --> 01:00:52,000
I think that's a huge…

1170
01:00:52,000 --> 01:00:53,000
I…

1171
01:00:53,000 --> 01:00:56,000
I'm telling you right now, we're abdicating some of our responsibilities.

1172
01:00:56,000 --> 01:00:57,000
That…

1173
01:00:57,000 --> 01:00:58,000
That's how I…

1174
01:00:58,000 --> 01:00:59,000
I feel very strongly about that.

1175
01:00:59,000 --> 01:01:02,000
So, we're going to amend it from adopt to support.

1176
01:01:02,000 --> 01:01:03,000
All right.

1177
01:01:03,000 --> 01:01:04,000
Any further discussion?

1178
01:01:04,000 --> 01:01:05,000
Yeah.

1179
01:01:05,000 --> 01:01:06,000
All right.

1180
01:01:06,000 --> 01:01:07,000
All right.

1181
01:01:07,000 --> 01:01:10,000
Now, would you be kind enough to read the motion?

1182
01:01:10,000 --> 01:01:11,000
Just to make sure.

1183
01:01:11,000 --> 01:01:12,000
Kelly's like, ah!

1184
01:01:12,000 --> 01:01:19,000
So, the around motion was, Mr. Cepia, a motion to adopt the changes to the middle school

1185
01:01:19,000 --> 01:01:22,000
UA schedule as delivered by Mitch Mentes tonight.

1186
01:01:22,000 --> 01:01:25,000
So, it will be Mr. Cepia supports…

1187
01:01:25,000 --> 01:01:26,000
Supported…

1188
01:01:26,000 --> 01:01:27,000
Supports to adopt…

1189
01:01:27,000 --> 01:01:28,000
Supportive.

1190
01:01:28,000 --> 01:01:29,000
Supportive.

1191
01:01:29,000 --> 01:01:30,000
Adopt needs to be taken out.

1192
01:01:30,000 --> 01:01:32,000
The middle school UA schedule as delivered by Mitch Mentes tonight.

1193
01:01:32,000 --> 01:01:33,000
Okay.

1194
01:01:33,000 --> 01:01:34,000
My second.

1195
01:01:34,000 --> 01:01:35,000
Adopt has to be taken out.

1196
01:01:35,000 --> 01:01:36,000
Yeah.

1197
01:01:36,000 --> 01:01:37,000
Okay.

1198
01:01:37,000 --> 01:01:38,000
Excellent.

1199
01:01:38,000 --> 01:01:39,000
All right.

1200
01:01:39,000 --> 01:01:40,000
Any other discussion?

1201
01:01:40,000 --> 01:01:41,000
All right.

1202
01:01:41,000 --> 01:01:42,000
All those in favor?

1203
01:01:42,000 --> 01:01:43,000
Opposed?

1204
01:01:43,000 --> 01:01:44,000
And abstain.

1205
01:01:44,000 --> 01:01:45,000
10-0-1.

1206
01:01:45,000 --> 01:01:46,000
Yeah.

1207
01:01:46,000 --> 01:01:48,000
Thank you all for coming.

1208
01:01:48,000 --> 01:01:50,000
My daughter's very excited for this.

1209
01:01:50,000 --> 01:01:52,000
Thank you guys for putting all the work in for it.

1210
01:01:52,000 --> 01:01:53,000
Awesome.

1211
01:01:53,000 --> 01:01:54,000
Have a good rest of your summer as you have.

1212
01:01:54,000 --> 01:01:55,000
Thanks, guys.

1213
01:01:55,000 --> 01:01:57,000
You started this by saying, I don't care which way the grass is mowing.

1214
01:01:57,000 --> 01:01:59,000
You don't care which way the grass is mowing.

1215
01:01:59,000 --> 01:02:00,000
And you've talked about it many times.

1216
01:02:00,000 --> 01:02:03,000
I care about the grass is growing.

1217
01:02:03,000 --> 01:02:04,000
Yeah.

1218
01:02:04,000 --> 01:02:05,000
All right.

1219
01:02:05,000 --> 01:02:18,000
Deliberative session update to kind of rewind and get everyone back.

1220
01:02:18,000 --> 01:02:22,000
I believe it starts on page 91 of your packets to bring you through the process.

1221
01:02:22,000 --> 01:02:24,000
You had visited the board of selecting with me in the spring.

1222
01:02:24,000 --> 01:02:28,000
We had asked for kind of their partnership and help in getting that survey out.

1223
01:02:28,000 --> 01:02:30,000
We had support from all four towns.

1224
01:02:30,000 --> 01:02:34,000
That survey was out and open for about two months and closed on June 30th.

1225
01:02:34,000 --> 01:02:36,000
The results are there in front of you.

1226
01:02:36,000 --> 01:02:39,000
525 submissions.

1227
01:02:39,000 --> 01:02:45,000
The breakdown generally across the board is about 25% of folks still wanting to opt for

1228
01:02:45,000 --> 01:02:52,000
a Thursday time, 13% of folks opting for a Friday time, and 62% of folks opting for

1229
01:02:52,000 --> 01:02:55,000
the Saturday time options that were available there.

1230
01:02:55,000 --> 01:02:58,000
You wanted to see this information and kind of get a dipstick of kind of where the community

1231
01:02:58,000 --> 01:03:03,000
is at and then discuss as a board maybe next steps as we think about what date we want

1232
01:03:03,000 --> 01:03:06,000
to be setting for the Deliberative session in 27.

